<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Avar Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1864 CE (approx.) · capital Khunzakh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Avar state of mountainous Dagestan for six centuries, centred on Khunzakh, whose khans were courted by Persia, the Ottomans and Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the decline of Christian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sarir">Sarir</a>, a ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> nutsals emerged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khunzakh">Khunzakh</a> around the 12th and 13th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread into the region during the 13th to 15th centuries, and the nutsals came to rule as Muslim khans over the Avar communities of the highlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans held direct power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khunzakh">Khunzakh</a> and nearby villages but relied on alliances with the self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> free communities, which had their own assemblies and customary law. Wealth came from herding, terraced farming and raiding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma-khan-v">Umma Khan</a>, who ruled from 1774, led raids deep into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a>, and the khanate received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1834 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamzat-bek">Hamzat Bek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caucasian-imamate">Caucasian Imamate</a>, massacred the khan's family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khunzakh">Khunzakh</a>. The khanate then passed to rulers installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a>'s forces from 1843 to 1859, and was abolished by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1864 and turned into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/avar-khanate.jpg" alt="Avar Khanate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Avars_(Variant_3).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khunzakh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Umma Khan, Pakhu-Bike</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Avar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Avaristan, Khanate of Avaria, Nutsal of Avaria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=avar-khanate" aria-label="Avar Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5811 665 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Avar Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6131 921L6130 920M6131 921L6281 1235M6131 921L6487 457M6131 921L6113 908"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6130 920h0M6281 1235h0M6487 457h0M6113 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6131 921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6131 921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khunzakh. <a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=avar-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
